WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:01:53.000
How the user centered design ideas came about: workshops with GPs, programmers and psychologists

2
00:01:53.000 --> 00:04:12.000
Photos from the workshops ('three ring circuses') - circular menus and radio buttons were a disaster; actors playing patients

3
00:04:12.000 --> 00:05:33.000
Rapid prototyping in Smalltalk - 'cardboard' interfaces built before the guts behind them; useful and usable

4
00:05:33.000 --> 00:06:59.000
Summarizing the medical record: problem lists, graphs and timelines - 'exhaustive is the enemy of the useful'

5
00:06:59.000 --> 00:08:21.000
Typing and Read codes by heart - fast paths for experienced users

6
00:08:21.000 --> 00:10:16.000
Tailoring to each doctor and practice - favourite prescriptions, and AI for personalization today?

7
00:10:16.000 --> 00:12:00.000
Different forms for different trials, CLEF - 'it's very easy to do things that aren't helpful'

8
00:12:00.000 --> 00:13:22.000
'Death by a thousand clicks' - throwing out a prototype isn't painful. Low code/no code prototyping today? 'Essential.'

9
00:13:22.000 --> 00:15:37.000
What works in general practice doesn't work everywhere: geriatrics, radiologists and light boxes

10
00:15:37.000 --> 00:17:29.000
One record system for the whole hospital and general practice? Modules and tailoring (cath lab, dermatology photos)

11
00:17:29.000 --> 00:21:56.000
Individual options per doctor, and typing or voice into a big tree - record exactly what was said, or offer alternatives?

12
00:21:56.000 --> 00:25:16.000
'Medicine is a humanly impossible task' and a wicked problem - errors, interruptions and reminders

13
00:25:16.000 --> 00:27:50.000
You don't need a computer to fix everything (incompatible gas connectors, coloured syringes) - but slow deterioration is hard to catch

14
00:27:50.000 --> 00:28:03.560
The end
